write_tree('CSCA 421', 'Computer Networks', 2, [
 ('I', 'Introduction to Networks and the Physical Layer', [
  ('Network Topology', 'Topology is the physical or logical arrangement of nodes and links in a network.',
   [('Bus topology', 'All nodes share one backbone cable, which is cheap but a single break stops the whole network.'),
    ('Star topology', 'Every node connects to a central hub or switch, so one cable failure affects only that node.'),
    ('Ring topology', 'Nodes form a closed loop and data circulates in one direction, with a token often controlling access.'),
    ('Mesh topology', 'Every node links to every other node, giving high reliability at high cabling cost; partial mesh is a compromise.'),
    ('Tree and hybrid topology', 'Hierarchical combinations of star and bus arrangements used in campus networks.'),
    ('LAN, MAN and WAN', 'Networks classified by reach: a room or building, a city, and a country or the world.')]),
  ('Network Architecture', 'Network architecture describes how the work of communication is divided among layers and how they interact.',
   [('Client server architecture', 'A server provides services such as file, web or mail access and clients request them.'),
    ('Peer to peer architecture', 'Every computer can act as both client and server, which suits small networks without a central machine.'),
    ('Layered architecture and protocols', 'Each layer offers services to the layer above and uses the layer below, communicating through protocols.'),
    ('Connection oriented versus connectionless service', 'A connection oriented service agrees on a path and guarantees order, while a connectionless service sends independent packets.')]),
  ('Reference Models', 'Reference models divide network functions into layers: the seven layer OSI model and the four or five layer TCP/IP model.',
   [('OSI model and its seven layers', 'Physical, data link, network, transport, session, presentation and application layers, each with defined duties.'),
    ('TCP/IP model', 'Network access, internet, transport and application layers, which is what the real Internet implements.'),
    ('Comparison of OSI and TCP/IP', 'OSI is a teaching reference with separate session and presentation layers; TCP/IP is practical and merges them into the application layer.'),
    ('Encapsulation and de-encapsulation', 'Each layer adds its own header on the way down and removes it on the way up, forming a protocol data unit.')]),
  ('Example Networks', 'ARPANET, NSFNET and the Internet show how research networks evolved into one global network.',
   [('ARPANET', 'The 1969 US defence research network that introduced packet switching and the first host protocols.'),
    ('NSFNET', 'A US academic backbone created in the 1980s that opened the network to universities and later to commercial traffic.'),
    ('The Internet', 'A network of networks using TCP/IP, governed by ISPs, IXPs and standard bodies such as IETF and ICANN.'),
    ('Other example networks', 'Public switched telephone networks, cable and mobile networks, and enterprise intranets.')]),
  ('Physical Layer', 'The physical layer transmits raw bits over a medium by defining voltages, frequencies, data rates and connectors.',
   [('Functions of the physical layer', 'Bit synchronisation, bit rate control, line configuration and physical topology.'),
    ('Bandwidth, data rate and latency', 'Bandwidth is the capacity in hertz or bits per second, while latency is the delay a signal suffers.'),
    ('Transmission modes', 'Simplex is one direction only, half duplex alternates and full duplex works both ways at once.'),
    ('Switching techniques', 'Circuit switching reserves a path, message switching stores and forwards whole messages and packet switching forwards small packets.'),
    ('Multiplexing', 'Frequency, time and wavelength division multiplexing share one medium among several signals.'),
    ('Transmission impairments', 'Attenuation, distortion and noise degrade the signal and set limits on distance and speed.')]),
  ('Transmission Media', 'Transmission media are the physical paths that carry signals, either guided along a cable or unguided through air.',
   [('Twisted pair cable', 'Two insulated copper wires twisted together; unshielded and shielded forms are cheap and easy to install but suffer interference.'),
    ('Coaxial cable', 'A central conductor with a braided shield, giving higher bandwidth and better noise immunity than twisted pair.'),
    ('Optical fibre', 'Glass or plastic core carrying light, offering very high bandwidth, low attenuation and immunity to electromagnetic interference.'),
    ('Radio waves and microwaves', 'Unguided media for broadcasting and line of sight links, affected by weather and obstacles.'),
    ('Infrared and satellite links', 'Infrared suits short indoor links while satellites cover wide areas with higher delay.'),
    ('Guided versus unguided media', 'Guided media confine the signal to a cable while unguided media transmit through free space.')])
 ]),
 ('II', 'The Data Link Layer', [
  ('Data Link Layer Design Issues', 'The data link layer turns a raw transmission line into a reliable link between two adjacent nodes.',
   [('Framing', 'Grouping the bit stream into frames using character counts, flag bytes with stuffing, or physical layer coding violations.'),
    ('Error control', 'Detecting and correcting damaged frames with acknowledgements, timers and retransmission.'),
    ('Flow control', 'Preventing a fast sender from drowning a slow receiver through feedback or rate based schemes.'),
    ('Link management and addressing', 'Establishing, maintaining and releasing links and identifying the physical addresses of neighbours.')]),
  ('Error Detection and Correction Methods', 'Errors are detected with redundant bits and corrected either by retransmission or by locating the error bits.',
   [('Types of errors', 'A single bit error flips one bit while a burst error damages a continuous group of bits.'),
    ('Parity check', 'Adds one bit so the number of ones is even or odd; simple but it can miss two bit errors.'),
    ('Two dimensional parity', 'Rows and columns are both checked, which can locate and correct a single bit error.'),
    ('Checksum', 'The sender appends the sum of the data words so the receiver can verify the total, commonly used in the Internet.'),
    ('Cyclic Redundancy Check (CRC)', 'Treats the frame as a polynomial and appends the remainder of division by a generator polynomial, giving strong detection.'),
    ('Hamming code', 'Inserts check bits at power of two positions so the receiver can identify and correct a single bit error.'),
    ('Hamming distance and code capability', 'The minimum number of bit changes between valid codewords decides how many errors can be detected or corrected.')]),
  ('Elementary Data Link Protocols', 'The elementary protocols add reliability step by step, starting from an ideal channel and moving to a noisy one.',
   [('Unrestricted simplex protocol', 'An ideal channel with no errors and no flow control, where the sender transmits endlessly.'),
    ('Stop and wait protocol', 'The sender sends one frame and waits for its acknowledgement before sending the next, which solves flow control.'),
    ('Simplex protocol for a noisy channel', 'Frames are numbered and retransmitted after a timeout or a negative acknowledgement, which needs timers and sequence numbers.'),
    ('Piggybacking', 'Attaching an acknowledgement to a data frame travelling in the opposite direction to save bandwidth.')]),
  ('Sliding Window Protocols', 'Sliding window protocols allow several frames to be outstanding, which keeps the link busy on long delay paths.',
   [('Sender and receiver windows', 'Each side keeps a window of sequence numbers; the window slides forward as acknowledgements arrive.'),
    ('One bit sliding window', 'Window size one on both sides, which behaves like stop and wait with numbered frames.'),
    ('Go Back N', 'The receiver discards out of order frames and the sender retransmits the whole window from the lost frame onwards.'),
    ('Selective Repeat', 'The receiver buffers correct frames and the sender retransmits only the missing ones, which needs a larger window and sorting logic.'),
    ('Efficiency and window size', 'Larger windows improve utilisation but need more sequence numbers; window size and bandwidth-delay product must match.')]),
  ('Protocol Verification Methods', 'Protocol verification proves that a protocol behaves correctly in every possible interaction between the two sides.',
   [('Finite state machine model', 'Each protocol machine is drawn as states and transitions, and the reachable product machine is checked for deadlock or unspecified states.'),
    ('Petri net model', 'Places and transitions model the flow of tokens representing messages and states.'),
    ('Channel and queue models', 'The communication channel is modelled as a queue with bounds to expose overflow or loss behaviour.'),
    ('Why verification matters', 'Protocols that seem correct can deadlock or lose frames in rare interleavings, which only formal checking reveals.')]),
  ('Channel Allocation and Multiple Access Protocols', 'Multiple access protocols decide which station may use a shared channel at a given moment.',
   [('ALOHA and slotted ALOHA', 'Stations transmit whenever they wish, with retransmission after collisions; slotted ALOHA doubles the throughput by aligning to time slots.'),
    ('Carrier Sense Multiple Access (CSMA)', 'A station listens before transmitting; persistent and non persistent variants differ in how they retry after sensing a busy channel.'),
    ('CSMA with Collision Detection (CSMA/CD)', 'Stations detect collisions while transmitting and abort early, which is the basis of classic Ethernet.'),
    ('CSMA with Collision Avoidance (CSMA/CA)', 'Used in wireless networks where collisions cannot be detected, so stations reserve the medium with acknowledgements and backoff.'),
    ('Collision free protocols', 'Bit map reservation, binary countdown and token passing avoid collisions entirely at the cost of overhead.'),
    ('Limited contention protocols', 'Adaptive tree walk and reservation schemes balance contention and order under varying load.')]),
  ('IEEE 802 Standards', 'The IEEE 802 family standardises local and metropolitan area network technologies.',
   [('IEEE 802.1 and 802.2', 'Cover bridging, VLAN tagging and the logical link control sublayer.'),
    ('IEEE 802.3 Ethernet', 'CSMA/CD with MAC addressing and frame formats; switched full duplex Ethernet is the dominant LAN technology today.'),
    ('IEEE 802.4 token bus and 802.5 token ring', 'Deterministic token passing networks with bounded access delay, now obsolete but still examined.'),
    ('IEEE 802.11 wireless LAN', 'Uses CSMA/CA with optional RTS/CTS handshaking; standards such as 802.11b, g, n, ac and ax differ in speed and band.'),
    ('Bridges, switches and VLANs', 'Link layer devices that forward frames by MAC address and can separate broadcast domains.')])
 ]),
 ('III', 'The Network Layer', [
  ('Network Layer Design Issues', 'The network layer delivers packets from source to destination across many intervening networks.',
   [('Store and forward packet switching', 'A router receives a packet, stores it, inspects the header and forwards it on the chosen link.'),
    ('Services offered to the transport layer', 'Datagram service treats packets independently, while virtual circuit service sets up a path before sending.'),
    ('Routing and forwarding', 'Routing builds the tables by which forwarding decisions are made hop by hop.'),
    ('Internetworking challenges', 'Different frame sizes, addressing schemes, and quality of service across heterogeneous networks.'),
    ('Congestion and QoS', 'When offered load exceeds capacity, delay and loss grow, so the layer must manage congestion.')]),
  ('Routing Algorithms', 'Routing algorithms choose the path a packet takes, either by a global view of the network or by exchanging information with neighbours.',
   [('Fixed and flooding routing', 'Fixed routing uses static tables while flooding sends every packet on all links except the incoming one.'),
    ('Shortest path (Dijkstra) routing', 'Builds a least cost tree from the source using the Dijkstra algorithm on a weighted graph.'),
    ('Distance vector routing', 'Each router tells its neighbours its distance to every destination, as in RIP; slow to converge and prone to count to infinity.'),
    ('Link state routing', 'Every router learns the full topology with link state packets and runs shortest path, as in OSPF.'),
    ('Path vector and BGP', 'Border routers exchange full path information, which detects loops and applies policy between autonomous systems.'),
    ('Hierarchical, broadcast and multicast routing', 'Areas reduce table size, flooding and spanning trees deliver broadcast, and multicast trees serve group traffic.')]),
  ('Congestion Control Algorithms', 'Congestion control keeps the number of packets inside the network below the level at which performance collapses.',
   [('Causes and effects of congestion', 'Queue build up increases delay and drops packets, and beyond a point throughput falls as load rises.'),
    ('Leaky bucket and token bucket', 'Traffic shaping smooths bursts by releasing packets at a fixed rate or by allowing saved credits for bursts.'),
    ('Choke packets and explicit signalling', 'A congested router asks the source to slow down, either by marking or by sending control packets.'),
    ('Load shedding and admission control', 'Under pressure the network drops some packets deliberately or refuses new flows.'),
    ('Flow control versus congestion control', 'Flow control protects the receiver while congestion control protects the network itself.')]),
  ('Internetworking and the Network Layer in the Internet', 'The Internet layer glues dissimilar networks together with IP addressing and supporting protocols.',
   [('IP addressing and classes', 'IPv4 addresses are grouped in classes A to E historically, with network and host parts.'),
    ('Subnetting and supernetting', 'Borrowing bits from the host part creates subnets, while CIDR aggregates prefixes to reduce table size.'),
    ('Network address translation', 'A private address space is mapped to one public address using port numbers, which eases address shortage.'),
    ('IPv4 datagram format', 'Version, header length, total length, identification, flags, fragment offset, TTL, protocol, checksum, source and destination addresses.'),
    ('Fragmentation and reassembly', 'Packets are split to fit a link MTU and reassembled at the destination using the identification field.'),
    ('IPv6', '128 bit addresses, fixed header, flow label, no header checksum and built in extension headers.'),
    ('ICMP, ARP and DHCP', 'ICMP reports errors and echo, ARP resolves IP to MAC and DHCP assigns addresses automatically.'),
    ('Routing protocols in the Internet', 'RIP inside small networks, OSPF inside large ones and BGP between autonomous systems.')])
 ]),
 ('IV', 'The Transport Protocols', [
  ('Transport Service', 'The transport layer provides end to end communication between processes on different hosts.',
   [('Services to the upper layers', 'Reliable or unreliable delivery, connection management, flow control and multiplexing.'),
    ('Transport service primitives', 'LISTEN, CONNECT, ACCEPT, SEND, RECEIVE, DISCONNECT and CLOSE describe how applications use the service.'),
    ('Sockets and port numbers', 'A socket is an IP address plus a port, which identifies one endpoint of a connection.'),
    ('Berkeley sockets API', 'socket, bind, listen, accept, connect, send, recv and close are the standard programming calls.')]),
  ('Transport Protocols and Their Elements', 'A transport protocol must handle addressing, connection setup, release, flow control and multiplexing.',
   [('Addressing and connection identifiers', 'Ports and connection identifiers distinguish simultaneous conversations between the same hosts.'),
    ('Connection establishment', 'Three way handshake, or a four way handshake when both sides open together.'),
    ('Connection release', 'Two way and four way release avoid losing data when both sides close at the same time.'),
    ('Flow control and buffering', 'Sliding window flow control buffers data at the receiver to match slow processes.'),
    ('Multiplexing and demultiplexing', 'Many applications share the network service through ports, which identifies the destination process.'),
    ('Crash recovery and timers', 'Retransmission timers must be set carefully, and a crashed host must not break later connections.')]),
  ('Internet Transport Protocols: UDP and TCP', 'UDP provides simple connectionless datagram service and TCP provides reliable connection oriented byte streams.',
   [('User Datagram Protocol (UDP)', 'Eight byte header with source port, destination port, length and checksum; no connection, no reliability, low overhead.'),
    ('Transmission Control Protocol (TCP)', 'Connection oriented, ordered and reliable, with flow control, congestion control and sequence numbers.'),
    ('TCP segment header', 'Source and destination ports, sequence and acknowledgement numbers, header length, flags, window size, checksum and options.'),
    ('Three way handshake', 'SYN, SYN ACK and ACK set up the sequence numbers and parameters before data flows.'),
    ('Sliding window flow control in TCP', 'The receiver advertises a window that tells the sender how much data may be outstanding.'),
    ('TCP congestion control', 'Slow start, congestion avoidance, fast retransmit and fast recovery adjust the sending rate after loss.'),
    ('TCP timers', 'Retransmission, persist, keep alive and two-army timers manage retransmission and dead connections.'),
    ('UDP versus TCP', 'UDP is fast, simple and used for streaming, DNS and games; TCP is reliable and used for web, mail and file transfer.')]),
  ('Performance Issues', 'Transport performance depends on delay, bandwidth, window size and the cost of connection setup.',
   [('Bandwidth and delay', 'The bandwidth-delay product decides how much data may be in flight, which sets the needed window size.'),
    ('Throughput and utilisation', 'Actual throughput is limited by window size, round trip time and congestion.'),
    ('Round trip estimation and jitter', 'RTT and its variation are estimated to set safe retransmission timeouts.'),
    ('Delayed and duplicate acknowledgements', 'Acknowledgement policy affects throughput and unnecessary retransmissions.'),
    ('Quality of service concerns', 'Delay sensitive applications such as voice need bounded latency and jitter, which TCP does not guarantee.')])
 ]),
 ('V', 'The Application Layer and Network Security', [
  ('Application Layer Design Issues', 'The application layer provides the services that users and programs actually use, such as web, mail and file transfer.',
   [('Client server and peer to peer models', 'Applications either ask a server for a service or share work among equal peers.'),
    ('Uniform naming and addressing', 'URLs, domain names and port numbers locate the resource and the service.'),
    ('Application protocols and APIs', 'Each service defines a message format and sequence of exchanges, exposed to programmers through an API.')]),
  ('Domain Name System', 'DNS translates human readable domain names into IP addresses through a distributed hierarchy of name servers.',
   [('Name space and hierarchy', 'The tree of names with root, top level domains, second level domains and hosts.'),
    ('Root, TLD, authoritative and local name servers', 'Each level answers for its own part of the tree, and resolvers cache answers to cut traffic.'),
    ('Name resolution', 'Recursive resolution makes the server do the work while iterative resolution returns the next server to ask.'),
    ('Resource records', 'A, AAAA, CNAME, MX, NS, SOA, TXT and PTR records hold the different kinds of name data.'),
    ('DNS in practice and security', 'Caching, TTL values and threats such as cache poisoning, addressed by DNSSEC.')]),
  ('Electronic Mail', 'Email is a store and forward service in which the server of the sender pushes messages to the mailbox of the recipient.',
   [('Architecture of email', 'User agents compose and read mail while mail servers transfer and store it.'),
    ('Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP)', 'Push protocol on port 25 used to send mail and to relay between servers.'),
    ('POP3 and IMAP', 'POP3 downloads and usually deletes messages from the server; IMAP keeps them on the server and syncs folders, which suits multiple devices.'),
    ('Message format and MIME', 'RFC 822 headers with From, To, Subject and the body, and MIME extends this to attachments, images and other character sets.'),
    ('Mail security', 'STARTTLS, SPF, DKIM and DMARC reduce spoofing and eavesdropping.')]),
  ('World Wide Web', 'The web is a collection of hyperlinked documents retrieved with HTTP and addressed by URLs.',
   [('URL and URI structure', 'Scheme, host, port, path, query and fragment identify a resource on a server.'),
    ('HTTP overview', 'A request response text protocol; HTTP is stateless, so cookies or tokens carry state.'),
    ('HTTP request methods', 'GET, POST, PUT, DELETE, HEAD and OPTIONS describe the intended action.'),
    ('HTTP response and status codes', '1xx informational, 2xx success, 3xx redirection, 4xx client error and 5xx server error.'),
    ('Persistent connections, cookies and caching', 'Keep alive reduces setup cost, cookies maintain sessions and cache headers reduce repeat transfers.'),
    ('Web servers and proxies', 'Servers such as Apache, Nginx and IIS serve content; proxies and CDNs cache and accelerate delivery.'),
    ('Web 2.0 and dynamic pages', 'AJAX, REST APIs and single page applications changed the web from static pages to interactive programs.')]),
  ('Other Applications', 'Besides the web and email, the application layer provides file transfer, remote login, directories and network management.',
   [('File Transfer Protocol (FTP)', 'Uses a control connection on port 21 and a separate data connection, with active and passive modes.'),
    ('Telnet and SSH', 'Telnet sends text in the clear while SSH encrypts the session and supports tunnelling.'),
    ('DHCP and directory services', 'DHCP leases IP configuration automatically and LDAP holds directory information such as users and groups.'),
    ('Network management (SNMP)', 'Managed devices store data in MIBs and agents report to a manager, with traps for events.')]),
  ('Network Security', 'Network security protects data in transit from unauthorised reading, modification and denial of service.',
   [('Security goals and attacks', 'Confidentiality, integrity, availability and authentication versus interception, modification, masquerading and denial of service.'),
    ('Firewalls', 'Packet filters, stateful inspection and application proxies control traffic between networks.'),
    ('Intrusion detection and VPNs', 'IDS and IPS watch for attacks, while VPN tunnels carry private traffic over public networks.')]),
  ('Basic Cryptography: Symmetric and Asymmetric', 'Cryptography protects messages by transforming them with keys; symmetric schemes share one key and asymmetric schemes use a key pair.',
   [('Basic terms', 'Plaintext, ciphertext, encryption, decryption, key and cryptanalysis.'),
    ('Substitution and transposition ciphers', 'Characters are replaced or rearranged; classical examples are the Caesar cipher and the rail fence cipher.'),
    ('Symmetric key cryptography', 'The same secret key encrypts and decrypts; DES uses 56 bit keys, 3DES repeats it, and AES uses 128 to 256 bit keys with faster and stronger security.'),
    ('Asymmetric key cryptography', 'A public key encrypts and the matching private key decrypts, as in RSA and elliptic curve cryptography, which also enables signatures.'),
    ('Digital signature and certificates', 'A hash encrypted with the private key proves origin and integrity, and a certificate authority vouches for public keys with X.509 certificates.'),
    ('Hash functions and message authentication', 'MD5 and SHA families produce fixed length digests; HMAC combines a hash with a shared secret.'),
    ('Symmetric versus asymmetric comparison', 'Symmetric is fast but needs key distribution, asymmetric solves distribution but is slow, so both are combined in TLS.')])
 ])
])

write_tree('CSCA 422', 'Operating Systems', 2, [
 ('I', 'Introduction, Processes and CPU Scheduling', [
  ('Types of Operating Systems', 'Operating systems are classified by how they manage jobs, users and time constraints.',
   [('Batch operating system', 'Jobs with similar needs are grouped and run without user interaction, which wastes time while waiting for I/O.'),
    ('Multiprogramming system', 'Several jobs stay in memory so the CPU can switch to another job while one waits for I/O.'),
    ('Time sharing (multitasking) system', 'The CPU switches rapidly among interactive users, giving each a small time slice.'),
    ('Real time system', 'Hard and soft real time systems must meet deadlines, used in control and embedded applications.'),
    ('Distributed and network operating systems', 'Distributed systems present many machines as one, while network systems let users log in to separate machines.'),
    ('Embedded and mobile operating systems', 'Small footprint systems for appliances, phones such as Android, and IoT devices.')]),
  ('Operating System Structures', 'The structure of an operating system describes how its components are organised and how they communicate.',
   [('Simple or monolithic structure', 'One large kernel in which any procedure can call any other, as in early MS-DOS and UNIX.'),
    ('Layered structure', 'The system is divided into levels, each using only the level below, which is easier to debug but slower.'),
    ('Microkernel structure', 'Only essential services stay in the kernel while others run as user processes, improving reliability and security.'),
    ('Modular and hybrid structure', 'Loadable kernel modules combine flexibility with speed; hybrid kernels mix monolithic and microkernel ideas.'),
    ('Virtual machine structure', 'A layer of software presents several virtual copies of the hardware to different operating systems.')]),
  ('System Components and Operating System Services', 'The main components manage processes, memory, files, I/O, storage, protection and networking, and the services are what users and programs need.',
   [('Process management', 'Creating, scheduling, synchronising and terminating processes and threads.'),
    ('Memory management', 'Tracking memory use, allocating and freeing space, and managing virtual memory.'),
    ('File and storage management', 'Creating, organising and protecting files, directories and secondary storage.'),
    ('I/O and networking management', 'Device drivers, buffering, caching and spooling for input output, plus protocols for communication.'),
    ('Protection and security', 'Controlling access to resources and defending against internal and external attacks.'),
    ('User services versus system services', 'User interfaces, program execution, I/O and file operations versus resource allocation, accounting and error detection.')]),
  ('System Calls, System Programs and Operating System Design', 'A system call is the programming interface to the kernel and system programs are utilities built on those calls.',
   [('How a system call works', 'A trap switches the CPU to kernel mode, the service routine runs and control returns with a result.'),
    ('Types of system calls', 'Process control, file management, device management, information maintenance, communication and protection.'),
    ('System programs', 'Compilers, editors, shells, utilities and command interpreters that users run directly.'),
    ('Kernel mode and user mode', 'Privileged instructions run only in kernel mode, which protects the system from user programs.'),
    ('Operating system design goals', 'Efficiency, reliability, maintainability and a clear interface between mechanism and policy.')]),
  ('Processes and Process Scheduling', 'A process is a program in execution with its own state, and the scheduler decides which process runs on the CPU.',
   [('Process concept and program versus process', 'The program is passive code while the process is the active execution with a program counter and data.'),
    ('Process control block (PCB)', 'Stores process id, state, program counter, registers, memory limits, open files and accounting information.'),
    ('Process states', 'New, ready, running, waiting and terminated, with transitions triggered by scheduler decisions and events.'),
    ('Scheduling queues and schedulers', 'Job queue, ready queue and device queues served by long term, short term and medium term schedulers.'),
    ('Context switch', 'Saving the state of the running process and loading another, which is pure overhead.'),
    ('Operations on processes', 'Creation with fork, execution with exec, termination, and waiting by the parent with wait.'),
    ('Co-operating processes and interprocess communication', 'Independent processes do not affect each other while co-operating ones share data through shared memory or message passing, including pipes and sockets.')]),
  ('CPU Scheduling', 'CPU scheduling decides the order in which ready processes get the processor, which decides throughput and response time.',
   [('Scheduling criteria', 'CPU utilisation, throughput, turnaround time, waiting time and response time.'),
    ('First Come First Served (FCFS)', 'Processes run in arrival order; simple but suffers the convoy effect with long jobs first.'),
    ('Shortest Job First (SJF)', 'Runs the process with the smallest next CPU burst, which is optimal for average waiting time but needs burst prediction.'),
    ('Shortest Remaining Time First (SRTF)', 'The preemptive version of SJF, in which a newly arrived shorter job takes over the CPU.'),
    ('Priority scheduling', 'The highest priority process runs, with the risk of starvation, which ageing can prevent.'),
    ('Round Robin (RR)', 'Each process gets a fixed time quantum in rotation, which suits time sharing; the quantum size decides the overhead.'),
    ('Multilevel queue and multilevel feedback queue', 'Separate queues for different classes of jobs and migration between queues to balance responsiveness and throughput.'),
    ('Multiple processor scheduling', 'Load sharing, processor affinity and symmetric multiprocessing decide how threads are spread over CPUs.')])
 ]),
 ('II', 'Process Synchronisation and Deadlocks', [
  ('Critical Section Problem', 'The critical section problem is ensuring that processes update shared data one at a time while still making progress.',
   [('Critical section and race condition', 'The critical section is the code that touches shared data, and a race condition is the wrong result caused by unsynchronised access.'),
    ('Three requirements of a solution', 'Mutual exclusion, progress and bounded waiting.'),
    ('Peterson solution', 'A two process software solution using a turn variable and a flag array.'),
    ('Hardware support', 'Disabling interrupts, test and set, and compare and swap instructions provide atomic primitives.')]),
  ('Semaphores', 'A semaphore is an integer variable accessed only through atomic wait and signal operations, used to control access to shared resources.',
   [('wait and signal operations', 'wait decrements the value and blocks if it becomes negative; signal increments it and wakes a waiting process.'),
    ('Binary and counting semaphores', 'Binary semaphores take values 0 and 1 like a lock, while counting semaphores track a pool of resources.'),
    ('Semaphore implementation and busy waiting', 'Blocking implementations use queues instead of spinning, which avoids wasting CPU time.'),
    ('Deadlock and starvation with semaphores', 'Wrong ordering of waits can deadlock, and unfair scheduling can starve a process.')]),
  ('Classical Problems of Synchronisation', 'Three classic problems model the standard patterns of producer consumer, reader writer and multiple resource competition.',
   [('Producer consumer (bounded buffer)', 'Producers wait when the buffer is full and consumers wait when it is empty, using empty, full and mutex semaphores.'),
    ('Readers writers problem', 'Several readers may read at once but a writer needs exclusive access; variants favour readers or writers.'),
    ('Dining philosophers problem', 'Five philosophers with five forks show deadlock and starvation; solutions include asymmetry and a waiter.'),
    ('Sleeping barber and other models', 'Further classical models used to study waiting, blocking and fairness.')]),
  ('Critical Regions and Monitors', 'Higher level constructs such as critical regions and monitors group synchronisation with the data they protect.',
   [('Conditional critical regions', 'A region declares the shared variable and its condition, letting the language handle mutual exclusion.'),
    ('Monitor concept', 'A module holding shared data and procedures with automatic mutual exclusion, plus condition variables wait and signal.'),
    ('Monitor solutions', 'The producer consumer and dining philosophers problems are solved more cleanly with monitors than with raw semaphores.'),
    ('Message passing as synchronisation', 'Processes synchronise by exchanging messages with send and receive primitives instead of sharing memory.')]),
  ('Deadlocks', 'A deadlock is a set of processes each waiting for a resource held by another, so none can proceed.',
   [('Deadlock characterisation and four necessary conditions', 'Mutual exclusion, hold and wait, no preemption and circular wait must all hold.'),
    ('Resource allocation graph', 'Processes and resources as nodes with request, assignment and claim edges; a cycle suggests deadlock.'),
    ('Deadlock prevention', 'Break one necessary condition: request all resources at once, allow preemption, or impose an ordering on resources.'),
    ('Deadlock avoidance and safe state', 'The system refuses an allocation that would leave an unsafe state; the Banker algorithm tests safety.'),
    ('Deadlock detection', 'Use a wait-for graph or a reduction algorithm to find existing deadlocks.'),
    ('Deadlock recovery', 'Kill processes, preempt resources or roll back to a checkpoint.'),
    ('Ignoring deadlocks', 'Some systems assume deadlocks are rare and handle them manually, as UNIX often does.')]),
  ('Threads and Multithreading Models', 'A thread is a lightweight unit of execution within a process, sharing memory with other threads of the same process.',
   [('Thread concept and thread control block', 'A thread has its own program counter, registers and stack, but shares code, data and open files.'),
    ('Benefits of threads', 'Responsiveness, resource sharing, economy and better use of multiprocessor systems.'),
    ('User level versus kernel level threads', 'User threads are managed in a library without kernel help, while kernel threads are scheduled by the operating system.'),
    ('Many to one model', 'Many user threads map to one kernel thread, which is fast but one blocking call blocks all.'),
    ('One to one model', 'Each user thread has a kernel thread, giving true concurrency at the cost of overhead.'),
    ('Many to many model', 'Many user threads share a smaller number of kernel threads, combining the benefits of both.'),
    ('Multithreading in practice', 'Thread libraries such as POSIX threads, Java threads and threading models of Windows and Linux.')])
 ]),
 ('III', 'Memory Management', [
  ('Memory Management Basics', 'Memory management keeps track of every byte of memory and decides which processes get it and when.',
   [('Address binding and its stages', 'Instructions and data are bound to addresses at compile time, load time or execution time.'),
    ('Logical and physical address space', 'The logical address is generated by the CPU and the physical address is the one the memory unit sees, related by the memory management unit.'),
    ('Memory management unit and relocation register', 'Hardware that adds a base value to every logical address to produce the physical address.'),
    ('Dynamic loading and linking', 'Routines are loaded or linked only when needed, which saves memory and improves start up time.'),
    ('Overlays and protection', 'Overlays keep only needed sections of a large program in memory, while base and limit registers protect processes from each other.')]),
  ('Contiguous Memory Allocation and Swapping', 'In contiguous allocation each process occupies one continuous block of memory, and swapping moves whole processes to disk and back.',
   [('Fixed and variable partitioning', 'Fixed partitions have set sizes while variable partitions are created on demand to fit each process.'),
    ('First fit, best fit and worst fit', 'Allocation strategies that choose the first, the tightest or the largest suitable hole in the free list.'),
    ('Internal and external fragmentation', 'Internal fragmentation wastes space inside an allocated block while external fragmentation leaves many small unusable holes.'),
    ('Compaction', 'Shifting processes together to merge holes, which costs time and is possible only with relocatable code.'),
    ('Swapping and the backing store', 'A whole process moves to disk and back, which is slow but frees memory for others.')]),
  ('Paging', 'Paging divides physical memory into fixed size frames and logical memory into pages of the same size, removing external fragmentation.',
   [('Pages, frames and page table', 'Each process keeps a page table mapping page numbers to frame numbers.'),
    ('Address translation', 'The logical address splits into a page number and an offset; the page table gives the frame and the offset locates the byte.'),
    ('Translation lookaside buffer (TLB)', 'A small associative cache of recent translations that avoids a memory lookup for every reference.'),
    ('Effective access time', 'The average access time combining the TLB hit ratio with the page table access time.'),
    ('Shared pages and protection', 'Read only pages such as library code can be shared by many processes with protection bits in the page table.'),
    ('Multilevel and inverted page tables', 'Hierarchical tables handle large address spaces while an inverted table keeps one entry per frame.')]),
  ('Segmentation', 'Segmentation divides a program into logical units such as code, stack and data, each with its own base and limit.',
   [('Segments and segment table', 'Each segment has a name, a length and a base address held in the segment table.'),
    ('Address translation in segmentation', 'The segment number selects the entry and the offset must be within the segment limit.'),
    ('Segmentation with paging', 'Segments are paged to remove external fragmentation while keeping the logical structure.'),
    ('Paging versus segmentation', 'Paging is invisible to the programmer and fixed size, segmentation reflects program structure and is variable size.')]),
  ('Virtual Memory and Demand Paging', 'Virtual memory lets a process run even when only part of it is in memory, by loading pages on demand and keeping the rest on disk.',
   [('Virtual memory concept and benefits', 'Programs larger than physical memory can run, more processes fit in memory and loading is faster.'),
    ('Demand paging and the valid-invalid bit', 'A page is brought in only when referenced; the valid bit marks which pages are actually in memory.'),
    ('Page fault handling', 'The trap finds the page on disk, finds a free frame, reads the page, updates the table and restarts the instruction.'),
    ('Copy on write', 'Parent and child share pages until one of them writes, which makes process creation cheap.'),
    ('Effective access time with page faults', 'The very high cost of a fault dominates the average access time, so fault rates must stay low.')]),
  ('Page Replacement', 'Page replacement decides which page to remove from memory when a needed page must be brought in.',
   [('FIFO page replacement', 'Replaces the oldest page, which is simple but can suffer Belady anomaly where more frames cause more faults.'),
    ('Optimal page replacement', 'Replaces the page used farthest in the future, giving the lowest fault rate but requiring future knowledge.'),
    ('LRU page replacement', 'Replaces the least recently used page, which approximates optimal behaviour and needs stack or counter hardware support.'),
    ('LRU approximations: reference bit and clock', 'The reference bit, additional reference bits and the second chance or clock algorithm approximate LRU cheaply.'),
    ('Counting and stack algorithms', 'LFU and MFU algorithms count references; stack algorithms such as LRU do not suffer Belady anomaly.'),
    ('Frame allocation policies', 'Equal, proportional and priority based allocation of frames among processes.')]),
  ('Thrashing', 'Thrashing is the state in which a system spends more time paging than executing useful work.',
   [('Causes of thrashing', 'Too many processes compete for too few frames, so each faults immediately after a page is replaced.'),
    ('Working set model', 'Tracks the pages used in the last delta references and gives each process enough frames for its working set.'),
    ('Page fault frequency control', 'Adds frames to a process with a high fault rate and removes frames from one with a very low rate.'),
    ('Locality of reference', 'Programs tend to reuse a small set of pages, which is why working set control works.')])
 ]),
 ('IV', 'Disk Scheduling, File Systems and Distributed File Systems', [
  ('Disk Structures and Disk Scheduling', 'A disk stores data on platters in tracks and sectors, and disk scheduling decides the order in which pending requests are served.',
   [('Disk structure', 'Platters, surfaces, tracks, sectors and cylinders, with the head moving on an arm and the platter spinning.'),
    ('Disk access time', 'Seek time, rotational latency and transfer time together decide the cost of a request.'),
    ('First Come First Served (FCFS)', 'Requests are served in arrival order, which is fair but causes long head movement.'),
    ('Shortest Seek Time First (SSTF)', 'Serves the nearest request, which improves throughput but can starve far requests.'),
    ('SCAN and C-SCAN', 'The head sweeps back and forth like an elevator; C-SCAN returns to the start without serving on the return trip for uniform waiting.'),
    ('LOOK and C-LOOK', 'Like SCAN and C-SCAN but the head reverses at the last request instead of the disk edge.'),
    ('Selecting a disk scheduling algorithm', 'Choice depends on load, fairness requirements and whether seek time dominates.')]),
  ('File System Interface', 'A file is a named collection of related information, and the file system interface defines how users and programs work with it.',
   [('File attributes and operations', 'Name, identifier, type, location, size, protection, timestamps; operations include create, open, read, write, seek, delete and truncate.'),
    ('File types and structure', 'Text and binary files, executable files and directories, with internal structure from none to indexed.'),
    ('Access methods', 'Sequential access reads in order, direct or relative access jumps to a block and indexed access uses an index of keys.'),
    ('Directory structures', 'Single level, two level, tree, and acyclic graph directories with path names and current directory.'),
    ('File sharing and protection', 'Access rights, access control lists and permission bits decide who may read, write or execute.')]),
  ('File System Implementation', 'Implementation covers how files, directories and free space are actually stored on disk.',
   [('File system layers and structures', 'Application, logical file system, file organisation module, basic file system, I/O control and devices.'),
    ('On disk structures', 'Boot control block, volume control block, directory structure and per file control block.'),
    ('In memory structures', 'Mount table, directory cache, system wide open file table and per process file table.'),
    ('Mounting and partitions', 'Volumes are mounted into the directory tree at a mount point, with optional virtual file system support.'),
    ('Directory implementation', 'A linear list of names and pointers or a hash table for faster lookup.'),
    ('Allocation methods', 'Contiguous allocation is fast but suffers fragmentation; linked allocation has no external fragmentation but slow random access; indexed allocation with index blocks supports direct access.'),
    ('Free space management', 'Bit vectors, linked lists, grouping and counting track which blocks are available.'),
    ('Efficiency and performance', 'Caching, buffering, read ahead and careful block size choice improve throughput.')]),
  ('Distributed File Systems', 'A distributed file system lets users access files stored on remote machines as if they were local.',
   [('Naming and transparency', 'Naming schemes map file names to locations; location transparency hides where the file is and location independence allows it to move.'),
    ('Remote file access', 'Remote service sends each request to the server while caching keeps copies locally; cache consistency must be managed.'),
    ('Stateful versus stateless service', 'A stateful server remembers client information between requests, which allows better performance but needs recovery; a stateless server treats each request independently.'),
    ('File replication', 'Copies of a file on several servers improve availability and performance but require update propagation.'),
    ('Case study: NFS and AFS', 'The network file system and the Andrew file system illustrate caching and session semantics.')])
 ]),
 ('V', 'Case Studies: Linux, Windows, Android and Virtual Machines', [
  ('Linux System', 'Linux is a free, open source, monolithic but modular UNIX like kernel with strong process and file management.',
   [('Design principles', 'Multiuser, multitasking, portable, modular and open source, with everything presented as a file.'),
    ('Kernel structure and modules', 'Loadable kernel modules extend the kernel at run time without reboot, and the shell is the user interface.'),
    ('Process management in Linux', 'fork and exec create processes, the scheduler uses a fair priority scheme, signals communicate with processes and process states resemble UNIX.'),
    ('File systems in Linux', 'The virtual file system layer supports ext4, XFS and others, with inodes, directories, links and mount points.'),
    ('Memory and device management', 'Virtual memory with demand paging and swap space, plus device drivers and special files.'),
    ('Users, groups and permissions', 'root and ordinary users, file permission bits and the sudo mechanism for privilege.')]),
  ('MS Windows System', 'Windows is a commercial, closed source operating system with a layered, object oriented environment and a hybrid kernel.',
   [('System structure', 'Hardware abstraction layer, kernel, executive services, device drivers, subsystems and the user mode environment.'),
    ('Objects, handles and registry', 'Executive objects manipulated through handles, with the registry storing system configuration.'),
    ('Process and thread management', 'Processes as containers of threads, the dispatcher database, priority based preemptive scheduling and fibres.'),
    ('Memory management in Windows', 'Demand paging on a 64 bit address space with working set management and a page frame database.'),
    ('File system and security', 'NTFS with journaling, compression and encryption, and an access token based security model.')]),
  ('Android OS', 'Android is a mobile operating system built on a modified Linux kernel with a Java like application framework.',
   [('Android architecture', 'Layers are the Linux kernel, hardware abstraction layer, native libraries with ART, Android runtime, application framework and applications.'),
    ('Application components', 'Activity, Service, Broadcast Receiver and Content Provider are the building blocks of an Android app.'),
    ('Activity and application life cycle', 'onCreate, onStart, onResume, onPause, onStop, onRestart and onDestroy manage state and resources.'),
    ('Dalvik and ART', 'Dalvik used just in time compilation from DEX bytecode while ART uses ahead of time and profile guided compilation.'),
    ('Security and permissions', 'Each app runs in its own sandbox under a unique user id and requests permissions for sensitive operations.')]),
  ('Virtual Machine Operating Systems', 'A virtual machine presents a complete hardware-like environment in software so several operating systems can share one physical machine.',
   [('Virtual machine concept', 'A layer of software creates an illusion of separate hardware for each guest operating system.'),
    ('Type 1 and type 2 hypervisors', 'Type 1 runs directly on hardware for servers, type 2 runs as an application on a host operating system.'),
    ('CPU, memory and I/O virtualisation', 'Privileged instructions are trapped or handled in hardware, memory is mapped with shadow or nested page tables, and devices are emulated or passed through.'),
    ('Benefits of virtualisation', 'Server consolidation, isolation, mobility of workloads, snapshots and easier testing.'),
    ('Containers versus virtual machines', 'Containers share the host kernel and isolate processes, which is lighter but less isolated than a full virtual machine.')])
 ])
])

write_tree('CSCA 423', 'Communication Skills', 2, [
 ('I', 'Communication Concepts and Process', [
  ('Communication: Concepts and Definition', 'Communication is the process of exchanging information, ideas and feelings between two or more people so that meaning is shared.',
   [('Definition and elements', 'Sender, message, encoding, channel, receiver, decoding, feedback and noise make up the communication process.'),
    ('Importance of communication', 'It coordinates work, builds relationships, supports decision making and prevents misunderstanding.'),
    ('Objectives of communication', 'To inform, persuade, instruct, motivate, warn, educate and build goodwill.')]),
  ('Process and Models of Communication', 'Models simplify the communication process and show where meanings can be lost.',
   [('Shannon and Weaver model', 'A linear model with source, encoder, channel, decoder, destination and noise.'),
    ('Berlo SMCR model', 'Source, message, channel and receiver, each influenced by skills, attitudes and knowledge.'),
    ('Transactional and interactive models', 'Communication is a simultaneous two way exchange in which both parties send and receive at once.'),
    ('Feedback and noise in the process', 'Feedback confirms understanding while noise - physical, semantic or psychological - distorts the message.')]),
  ('Types and Modes of Communication', 'Communication is classified by the medium used and the direction of flow.',
   [('Verbal and non verbal communication', 'Words carry the content while tone, gestures and expressions carry much of the meaning.'),
    ('Oral and written communication', 'Oral is fast and personal with immediate feedback; written is permanent, precise and easier to audit.'),
    ('Formal and informal communication', 'Formal follows the official chain of command while informal flows through the grapevine.'),
    ('Interpersonal and intrapersonal communication', 'Interpersonal is between two or more people while intrapersonal is the internal conversation within oneself.'),
    ('One way and two way communication', 'Two way communication includes feedback and therefore reduces misunderstanding.')]),
  ('Barriers to Communication and the Commandments of Communication', 'Barriers are obstacles that distort or block a message, and the commandments are the basic rules that prevent them.',
   [('Physical and environmental barriers', 'Noise, distance, poor network connection and uncomfortable surroundings.'),
    ('Semantic and language barriers', 'Jargon, poor vocabulary, bad grammar and words with several meanings.'),
    ('Psychological and emotional barriers', 'Prejudice, fear, lack of attention, stress and distrust.'),
    ('Organisational and cultural barriers', 'Long chains of command, information overload, status differences and cultural differences.'),
    ('Commandments of good communication', 'Be clear, concise, complete, correct, courteous, concrete and considerate - the seven C principles.')])
 ]),
 ('II', 'Developing Communication Skills: Reading, Writing, Listening and Speaking', [
  ('Reading Skills', 'Reading is the active skill of deriving meaning from written symbols, and it can be made faster and more purposeful.',
   [('Preparation before reading', 'Define the purpose, skim the layout, and decide what information is needed.'),
    ('Reading styles', 'Skimming for the gist, scanning for a specific fact, intensive reading for detail and extensive reading for pleasure.'),
    ('Linear and faster reading', 'Linear reading follows the text from start to finish while faster reading uses eye movement control and avoids sub-vocalisation.'),
    ('Reading techniques', 'SQ3R - survey, question, read, recite and review - along with note making, underlining and summarising.')]),
  ('Writing Skills', 'Effective writing is clear, correct and organised for a known reader and a defined purpose.',
   [('Principles of effective writing', 'Clarity, brevity, accuracy, coherence and appropriate tone, with a logical introduction, body and conclusion.'),
    ('Report writing', 'Formal documents with title, terms of reference, procedure, findings, conclusions and recommendations.'),
    ('Speech writing', 'Written to be heard, with a strong opening, signposted body and memorable conclusion.'),
    ('Minutes and agenda writing', 'The agenda lists items to be discussed and the minutes record decisions, attendance and action points.'),
    ('Letters and correspondence', 'Formal letters follow a fixed layout with sender address, date, receiver address, subject, salutation, body and closing.'),
    ('Article writing', 'Explains or argues a point for a general reader with a headline, introduction, main points and conclusion.'),
    ('Improving English language writing', 'Build vocabulary, revise grammar, read good writing and edit ruthlessly.'),
    ('When to write and when not to write', 'Write when a record, precision or evidence is needed; speak when urgency or discussion is needed.')]),
  ('Listening Skills', 'Listening is the conscious effort to hear and understand a message, and it is the most used communication skill.',
   [('Importance and advantages of listening', 'Good listening builds trust, reduces errors, improves decisions and saves the time lost in repeated work.'),
    ('Art of listening', 'Attention, patience, eye contact, avoiding interruption and reflecting the meaning back.'),
    ('Types of listening', 'Active, passive, empathetic, critical, appreciative and discriminative listening.'),
    ('Barriers to listening and listening tests', 'Distraction, premature evaluation, boredom and bias; listening tests use passages followed by questions to measure comprehension.')]),
  ('Speaking Skills', 'Speaking is the process of conveying ideas orally with clarity, confidence and suitable tone.',
   [('Art of conversation', 'Starting, maintaining and closing a conversation, taking turns, showing interest and avoiding argument.'),
    ('Telephone and telephonic etiquette', 'Identify yourself, speak clearly, keep it brief, listen actively and close politely.'),
    ('Methods of asking questions', 'Open, closed, leading, probing and rhetorical questions achieve different purposes.'),
    ('Brainstorming and presenting reports', 'Generate many ideas without judgement first, then organise and present the chosen ones with evidence.'),
    ('Improving speech delivery', 'Pace, pause, pitch, volume, pronunciation and the avoidance of filler words.'),
    ('Expressing techniques', 'Choosing the right words and tone, using examples and being concise.')])
 ]),
 ('III', 'Interview Techniques and Group Discussion', [
  ('Interview Techniques', 'An interview is a planned conversation in which an employer and a candidate assess each other.',
   [('What an interview is and why it matters', 'It verifies the application, checks communication and attitude, and gives the employer evidence beyond the resume.'),
    ('Types of interviews', 'Structured, unstructured, semi structured, panel, group, stress, telephonic, video and walk in interviews.'),
    ('Understanding the intricacies', 'Interviewers look for knowledge, clarity of thought, attitude and cultural fit as well as qualifications.'),
    ('Planning for an interview', 'Research the organisation and role, prepare examples with STAR - situation, task, action and result - and plan logistics.'),
    ('Answering skills', 'Listen fully, answer to the point, structure with examples and never bluff.'),
    ('Effective communication during interviews', 'Dress appropriately, keep good posture and eye contact, speak at a measured pace and thank the panel.'),
    ('TIPS to remember', 'Think before answering, be honest, present yourself confidently and stay positive about past employers.'),
    ('Mock interview', 'A practice session that builds confidence and exposes weak answers before the real thing.')]),
  ('Group Discussion', 'A group discussion is a purposeful conversation among several candidates evaluated for content, communication and teamwork.',
   [('Purpose of group discussion', 'To assess knowledge, reasoning, leadership, listening and the ability to work in a team.'),
    ('Process and getting started', 'Introduction of the topic, individual opinions, agreement building and conclusion within a time limit.'),
    ('Preparation', 'Read widely, keep facts and statistics ready and practise speaking in groups.'),
    ('Art of guiding and controlling discussion', 'Enter early and politely, keep the group on track and manage dominant speakers.'),
    ('Personality test through group discussion', 'Evaluators observe initiative, cooperation, patience and clarity under pressure.'),
    ('Lateral thinking', 'Looking at the same problem from an unexpected angle, which makes contributions memorable.'),
    ('Participation techniques and mock GD', 'Speak to the group rather than to the evaluator, build on others ideas, and practise with mock discussions.')])
 ]),
 ('IV', 'Body Language, Negotiation and Meetings', [
  ('Body Language', 'Body language is non verbal communication through posture, gestures, facial expression, eye contact and space.',
   [('Origin and development of body language', 'Inherited and learned signals that developed before speech as a way to convey danger, interest and intent.'),
    ('Body language as a tool for personality identification', 'Habits such as posture, handshake and eye contact give clues about confidence and attitude.'),
    ('Analysis of body language', 'The main areas are kinesics or gestures, proxemics or space, haptics or touch, oculesics or eye contact, and paralanguage or voice.'),
    ('Types of body language', 'Positive and open signals versus negative and closed signals such as folded arms and avoiding eye contact.'),
    ('Desirable body language', 'An open posture, steady eye contact, relaxed gestures, an appropriate distance and a genuine smile.'),
    ('Attitude and body language', 'Attitudes shape posture and expression, and changing posture can in turn influence attitude.'),
    ('Body language as powerful communication', 'Consistency between words and body language builds credibility; mismatch signals deception.')]),
  ('Negotiation Techniques', 'Negotiation is the process of reaching an agreement between parties with partly different interests.',
   [('Meaning and importance', 'Settling prices, salaries, contracts and resources without damaging relationships.'),
    ('Fundamentals of negotiation', 'Interests rather than positions, the zone of possible agreement and the best alternative to a negotiated agreement.'),
    ('Preparation and techniques', 'Know your limits and alternatives, set objectives, decide the opening offer and consider integrative options.'),
    ('Managing the process of negotiation', 'Explore, signal, propose, bargain and close, with active listening throughout.'),
    ('Interpersonal behaviour and case studies', 'Trust, patience, emotional control and cultural sensitivity decide outcomes.'),
    ('Mock negotiation', 'Practice sessions that reveal how concessions and tactics affect the final agreement.')]),
  ('Meetings and Seminars', 'A meeting is a planned gathering of people to discuss and decide, and a seminar is a presentation followed by discussion.',
   [('Meaning, importance and objectives', 'Meetings coordinate work, share information, solve problems and record decisions.'),
    ('Leading and participating in meetings', 'The chair sets the agenda and keeps time while participants prepare, contribute and listen.'),
    ('Success indicators and pitfalls', 'A clear decision, assigned actions and a closing summary indicate success; lack of agenda or domination by one person causes failure.'),
    ('Process of meetings', 'Notice, agenda, quorum, discussion, resolution, minutes and follow up.'),
    ('Communication skills for meetings', 'Concise speaking, note taking, constructive disagreement and summarising.'),
    ('Mock meetings and seminars', 'Simulated practice that builds the confidence to speak in formal settings.')])
 ]),
 ('V', 'Management Communication and Presentations', [
  ('Management Communication Relationships', 'Management communication is the flow of information that coordinates work inside an organisation.',
   [('Communication in management', 'Planning, organising, leading and controlling all depend on timely and accurate communication.'),
    ('Semantics and meaning', 'The same word can mean different things to different people, which creates misunderstanding.'),
    ('Employee and employer communication', 'Grievance handling, feedback, appraisal discussion and suggestion schemes.'),
    ('Downward and upward communication', 'Downward carries instructions, policies and feedback; upward carries reports, suggestions and complaints.'),
    ('Lateral, diagonal and informal communication', 'Peer communication between departments, cross level communication and the grapevine.'),
    ('Communication by specialists', 'Technical and staff specialists translate expert knowledge for the rest of the organisation.'),
    ('The union role in communication', 'Unions represent workers in negotiations and carry collective concerns to management.')]),
  ('Presentations', 'A presentation is a structured talk supported by visuals that informs, persuades or trains an audience.',
   [('Meaning and types of presentation', 'Informative, persuasive, instructional, motivational and impromptu presentations.'),
    ('Understanding the audience', 'Audience size, knowledge level, expectations and attitude decide content and tone.'),
    ('Planning and designing the presentation', 'Define the objective, build a clear structure and choose suitable visuals for each part.'),
    ('Written and oral presentation', 'A written report allows detail and rereading while an oral talk allows immediate interaction.'),
    ('Making use of notes and outlines', 'Cue cards and a short outline keep the speaker on track without reading out a script.'),
    ('Techniques for delivering presentation', 'Strong opening, eye contact, voice modulation, controlled pace, purposeful gestures and a clear conclusion.'),
    ('Personal style and model presentation', 'Developing a natural style and studying a model presentation to improve delivery.')])
 ])
])
